WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
Bash scripts often begin as small convenience tools.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:11.800
A developer writes a few lines to collect a status report, move a file, restart a service, rotate a log, or call an internal API.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:21.740
Over time, that helper becomes part of a deployment process, a scheduled maintenance job, a backup routine, a support workflow, or a remote administration task.

4
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:28.880
Once a script is operating real systems, it deserves the same attention to access control that we would give to any other production automation.

5
00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:39.140
The runtime identity may be able to read unrelated files, change unrelated services, access production credentials, or write into directories the script should never touch.

6
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.300
The access problem usually appears quietly.

7
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:54.240
A script may run from an administrator workstation, a privileged shell, a CI/CD runner, a cron entry, a service account, or a system service that already has broad rights.

8
00:00:54.340 --> 00:01:07.620
The author may only need one narrow action, but the runtime identity may be able to read unrelated files, change unrelated services, access production credentials, or write into directories the script should never touch.

9
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:12.440
The script works, but it works with far more power than its purpose requires.

10
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.320
Least privilege means giving the script only the access required for its specific job.

11
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.940
It is not about making automation painful or fragile.

12
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.360
It is about reducing blast radius.

13
00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:36.460
If a script has a bug, receives unexpected input, runs in the wrong directory, handles a malformed file, or is misconfigured by a future maintainer, limited access keeps the problem smaller.

14
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.640
The script may still fail, but it is less likely to damage unrelated systems or expose unrelated data.

15
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.000
The defensive goal is enough access for the job, no more.

16
00:01:47.100 --> 00:02:02.700
That means defining the script's purpose, selecting an appropriate execution identity, granting narrow file and command permissions, separating privileged actions from ordinary work, scoping credentials, controlling runtime context, and reviewing access over time.

17
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.760
In good Bash automation, privilege is deliberate.

18
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:11.940
It is visible enough to review, narrow enough to defend, and practical enough for operators to trust under pressure.

19
00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:19.320
Privilege is visible enough to review, narrow enough to defend, and practical enough for operators to trust under pressure.
